WEBVTT

00:00:08.819 --> 00:00:14.920
Today I'm going to look at the results of
our customer survey. First I'll go through

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:22.069
the survey questions, then summarise the results,
and finally I'll outline the conclusions.

00:00:22.069 --> 00:00:27.930
After that, there'll be time for questions
and discussion. So, let's start with the survey

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.719
questions.

00:00:30.719 --> 00:00:37.169
Turning to the results, as you can see from
the diagram, most people decided what to buy

00:00:37.169 --> 00:00:42.919
when they saw the product at the showroom.
About one third made their decision based

00:00:42.919 --> 00:00:49.879
on what the salesperson said. The others knew
what they wanted to buy already. Most of those

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:55.590
made their decision on the recommendation
of a friend. Only a few said they relied on

00:00:55.590 --> 00:01:00.150
advertising.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:07.090
Let's move on to the conclusions. The first
one is that it's very important that salespeople

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:15.209
on the floor know about our products. Another
is that after-sales service is critical. People

00:01:15.209 --> 00:01:22.629
who experience good after-sales service are
more likely to recommend a brand. And finally,

00:01:22.629 --> 00:01:34.870
advertising - it's expensive, so we need to
make sure we're getting results.

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:42.590
In today's program, we look again at a presentation.
Tan is presenting the results of a survey.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:48.129
Let's see how he does it. How does Tan start
his presentation?

00:01:48.129 --> 00:01:52.989
Today I'm going to look at the results of
our customer survey.

00:01:52.989 --> 00:01:58.290
When giving a presentation it's important
to state clearly what you are going to talk

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:02.450
about at the beginning. What is your topic?

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:10.610
For this, Tan uses the future tense, "I'm
going to". He could also have said, "I will".

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:14.380
And instead of 'look at' he could have used
other words:

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.939
examine
analyse

00:02:16.939 --> 00:02:18.909
review
discuss

00:02:18.909 --> 00:02:24.769
After introducing the topic, what does Tan
do next?

00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:31.109
First I'll go through the survey questions,
then summarise the results, and finally I'll

00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:33.120
outline the conclusions.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.240
After that, there'll be time for questions
and discussion.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:43.650
Tan outlines the structure of his presentation.
There were three parts. Notice how he signals

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:51.089
this by using sequencing words: first, then,
and finally.

00:02:51.089 --> 00:02:58.499
The structure of his talk is:
Introduction, then part 1, survey questions

00:02:58.499 --> 00:03:04.919
part 2, survey results
part 3, survey conclusions

00:03:04.919 --> 00:03:10.320
There's one more sequencing signal in his
introduction. Did you hear it?

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.449
After that, there'll be time for questions
and discussion.

00:03:13.449 --> 00:03:19.609
Even though Tan said 'finally' he would talk
about conclusions, he has something 'after

00:03:19.609 --> 00:03:25.930
that'. This is because the questions are not
part of his presentation. He's telling his

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:32.519
audience that after he's talked about conclusions,
it will be time to ask questions.

00:03:32.519 --> 00:03:38.760
So sequencing words are very useful - they
tell your audience how many parts are in your

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:46.089
talk - and they can signal when you are moving
from one topic to the next one. Sequencing

00:03:46.089 --> 00:03:58.189
words are words like firstly, secondly, thirdly,
then, next, finally, after that, following

00:03:58.189 --> 00:04:05.519
that, and later on. Another type of signal
can be used to show you are moving from one

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:08.510
part of your talk to another.

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:13.230
Here are three that Tan uses - practise them
with him.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:19.050
So, let's start with the questions

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:23.869
Turning to the results

00:04:23.869 --> 00:04:32.220
Let's move on to the conclusions
When we speak in English, pauses and intonation

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:39.780
are as important as the words we use - because
they help people understand. Listen:

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:43.600
Let's move on to the conclusions. The first
one is that it's very important that salespeople

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:47.959
on the floor know about our products. Another
is that after-sales service is critical.

00:04:47.959 --> 00:04:55.899
Without pauses or intonation, it's much harder
to understand - and it sounds boring. Let's

00:04:55.899 --> 00:05:00.480
add pauses.
Let's move on to the conclusions.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.209
The first one
is that it's very important

00:05:03.209 --> 00:05:07.160
that salespeople on the floor
know about our products.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:16.290
Another is that after-sales service is critical.
Pauses should come between sentences - here.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:22.540
But you'll notice small pauses in the middle
of sentences - after phrases. These help the

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:26.079
listener to follow what is being said.

00:05:26.079 --> 00:05:33.550
Now we add intonation and stress.
Let's move on to the conclusions. The first

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:41.630
one is that it's very important that salespeople
on the floor know about our products. Another

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:49.690
is that after-sales service is critical.
Intonation is the way we pronounce sentences.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:57.089
Note the downward intonation at the end of
sentences, "Let's move on to the conclusions",

00:05:57.089 --> 00:06:02.739
"about our products", "After sales service
is critical."

00:06:02.739 --> 00:06:10.260
Stress occurs in words, and sentences. In
words, one syllable is stressed. The wrong

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:20.470
stress makes it hard to understand. So:
conclusion, not conclusion

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:24.179
products, not products

00:06:24.179 --> 00:06:30.089
Even more important in speaking, is to stress
the important words in a sentence. This helps

00:06:30.089 --> 00:06:35.359
the meaning of what you are saying - it gives
emphasis.

00:06:35.359 --> 00:06:43.709
So Tan says, "Let's move on to the conclusions,"
stressing 'conclusions' because it's the key

00:06:43.709 --> 00:06:50.790
word in this sentence. The other words stressed
are the key words for understanding.

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:58.919
Let's listen to Tan once more, noting the
pauses, intonation, word and sentence stress.

00:06:58.919 --> 00:07:06.049
Let's move on to the conclusions. The first
one is that it's very important that salespeople

00:07:06.049 --> 00:07:15.519
on the floor know about our products. Another
is that after-sales service is critical.

00:07:15.519 --> 00:07:20.170
Let's look at the diagram, and how we can
describe numbers, or statistics.

00:07:20.170 --> 00:07:28.030
First, Tan says, "most people decided what
to buy at the showroom".

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:33.950
Because more people decided at the showroom
than at home, we can say most,

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:37.619
the majority, or over half.

00:07:37.619 --> 00:07:42.810
To describe people deciding at home, which
is less than fifty percent, we could say a

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:47.160
minority or less than half .

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:52.980
Looking at the reasons for decisions, we are
comparing four groups of people. We can use

00:07:52.980 --> 00:08:01.089
descriptive words such as many, some, a few.
And we can say the greatest number or the

00:08:01.089 --> 00:08:03.720
highest percentage .

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:08.989
The greatest number of people went by the
salesperson's recommendation.

00:08:08.989 --> 00:08:13.100
We could say only a few relied on advertising.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:22.679
And we can use words like approximately, about,
nearly, over and under.

00:08:22.679 --> 00:08:26.320
approximately one third
about a quarter

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:31.590
over a quarter
under a third

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:38.169
Finally, let's look at Tan's conclusions.
The first one is that it's important that

00:08:38.169 --> 00:08:46.779
salespeople on the floor know about our products.
Another is that after-sales service is critical.

00:08:46.779 --> 00:08:52.729
People who experience good after-sales service
are more likely to recommend a brand. And

00:08:52.729 --> 00:09:00.839
finally, advertising - it's expensive, so
we need to make sure we're getting results.

00:09:00.839 --> 00:09:06.990
Notice again how Tan uses signals for his
conclusions. His audience can clearly hear

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:08.640
that there are three.

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:12.770
He says:
the first one

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:17.060
another
and finally

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:22.680
Notice also how Tan uses adjectives to make
his points. You shouldn't use the same words

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:24.190
all the time.

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:34.770
What are the adjectives?
They are important, critical and expensive.

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:39.200
So, to summarise:
State your topic.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:45.330
Outline the structure of your presentation.
Use signalling and sequencing words.

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:51.630
Pay attention to intonation and stress.
Use descriptive words and adjectives, not

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:56.469
just numbers.
And in conclusion, that's all today on The

00:09:56.469 --> 00:09:57.420
Business of English.